## 8. Consider Attention Weight

Agents are subject to a concept called “attention weight.” If you’ve ever been reading a beefy novel and thought, “Wait, I don’t even remember who this character is” upon starting a new chapter, you’ve been there. The further an agent gets from a task’s origin point, the more likely it is to forget — or even deliberately ignore — instructions. 

Since an agent’s attention is finite, you want to be mindful of how much you’re asking it to do. If you’re noticing noncompliant output, consider adding a stop point in the backend instructions and having the user feed the agent a prompt on how to continue. LLMs are inherently conversational, and they place tremendous importance on user interaction. By saving a prompt the user can feed the agent, you “reset” the attention weight at that point in the process, minimizing drift and easing overall cognitive load. 

## 9. Watch Your Instruction Length

It turns out that there is a limit to how many instructions we can give a single agent, and that limit is 64,491 characters. Go over by a single period, and TeamAI will refuse to save your prompt. 

If you run over the limit without accomplishing everything an agent is meant to do, it may be time to look at multiagent orchestration, move information to a datastore, or incorporate agent skills instead. 

## And Finally, Talk to the Machine

Who knows Claude better than Claude? If a prompt or one of its pieces is behaving unexpectedly, open a chat with a high-power edition of whatever model you’re building on, then lead with something like:

“Hi! I've built an agent that [task]. When run, it [describe behavior]. Can you evaluate this portion of the prompt I've included below and determine what might be the issue? <prompt> </prompt>”

You can also give it the entire prompt and ask how it could be made faster and more stable. You don’t have to take every recommendation, but you’ll likely find a few good ones!

When discussing how to improve an agent with a chat model, it often helps to provide contextual information, such as:

- How the agent should be behaving.
- Whether any datastores or resources are involved — if so, you may want to provide them.
- What tools the agent is using to accomplish a goal — if the agent needs to run a retrieve, make sure your chat assistant is clear on this so it doesn’t provide instructions that limit external actions in an effort to improve speed and cut token use.
